1. RUN-TO-COMPLETION WORK
-------------------------
Deployments keep things running forever. Some work should run, finish,
and stop: database migrations, report generation, data imports,
backups, sending a batch of emails, ML training. That's a JOB.


2. JOB YAML
-----------
  apiVersion: batch/v1
  kind: Job
  metadata:
    name: report
  spec:
    completions: 1              # how many successful pods needed
    parallelism: 1              # how many at the same time
    backoffLimit: 4             # retries before the Job fails
    activeDeadlineSeconds: 600  # kill if it runs longer
    ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 3600   # auto-delete Job + pods after 1h
    template:
      spec:
        restartPolicy: Never    # or OnFailure (Always is not allowed)
        containers:
        - name: report
          image: python:3.12-slim
          command: ["python", "-c", "print('report done')"]


3. JOB PATTERNS
---------------
Single job               completions 1, parallelism 1
Fixed count              completions 10, parallelism 3
                         -> 10 successful runs, 3 at a time
Work queue               parallelism N, completions unset; pods pull
                         from a queue until empty (Week 9 with KEDA)
Indexed job              completionMode: Indexed; each pod gets
                         JOB_COMPLETION_INDEX 0..N-1 (process shard N)


4. RETRIES AND FAILURE
----------------------
restartPolicy: Never      failed pod stays; a NEW pod is created
                          (you can read logs of each attempt)
restartPolicy: OnFailure  same pod, container restarted in place
backoffLimit counts failures; delay grows 10s, 20s, 40s ... (max 6m).
When exceeded -> Job condition Failed, reason BackoffLimitExceeded.
Pods are kept for debugging until the Job is deleted (or TTL).

podFailurePolicy (advanced): e.g. don't retry on exit code 42
(a real bug), but do retry when the node disappears.


5. CRONJOB
----------
Creates a Job on a schedule (standard cron syntax, in the
kube-controller-manager's time zone, usually UTC, or set timeZone):

  apiVersion: batch/v1
  kind: CronJob
  metadata:
    name: nightly-backup
  spec:
    schedule: "30 2 * * *"         # 02:30 every day
    timeZone: "Asia/Kolkata"
    concurrencyPolicy: Forbid      # Allow | Forbid | Replace
    startingDeadlineSeconds: 300
    successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
    failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
    suspend: false
    jobTemplate:
      spec:
        template:
          spec:
            restartPolicy: OnFailure
            containers: [...]

Cron syntax: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week
  "*/5 * * * *"   every 5 minutes
  "0 * * * *"     every hour
  "0 9 * * 1-5"   09:00 on weekdays

concurrencyPolicy:
  Allow    runs may overlap (default)
  Forbid   skip the new run if the previous is still running
  Replace  kill the old run, start the new one


6. MAKE JOBS IDEMPOTENT
-----------------------
A Job pod may run more than once (retries, node failure). Design the
work so running twice is safe (e.g. "create table if not exists",
upserts, write to a temp file then rename).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Job = run to completion; restartPolicy Never or OnFailure.
- completions / parallelism / backoffLimit / ttlSecondsAfterFinished.
- CronJob = Job on a schedule; concurrencyPolicy controls overlap.
- Jobs must be safe to run twice.
